Z||000033||Raydar||06-17-2000||12:18 PM||||Terry,<BR>If your new radio has a flat connector, you will need a wiring harness adapter. Fieros, and other older GM radios, used four larger separate plugs for their connections. One for power/ground, one for front speakers, one for rear speakers, and one small connector for clock and radio memory/dimmer.<BR>I understand that the adapter is available at GM dealers.||||reg||1
Z||000034||KnightRyder31||06-17-2000||01:21 PM||||Yo Low, I live in Waterford, Mi. Thats right by Pontiac...no, I dont live in ghetto land.||||reg||1
Z||000035||tgowens||06-17-2000||02:58 PM||||Raydar, the original radio in my '87GT only has the one large GM plug -- I used an adapter while I had the Kenwood am/fm/cd player in there but the wiring harness in now uses the larger connector into the radio and the other smaller connectors to the sub-woofer (stock) and the overhead slider switch........not sure what the newer radio uses but if I'm not mistaken, it uses the larger single connector on the original harness -- I guess i'll find out when I install it -- oh well, I have been known to wire direct before -- checking continuity all the way!!||||reg||1
